## the zimmermann telegram


on march 1, 1917, a secret message was made public.


it was called the **zimmermann telegram**.


this event made tensions between germany and the united states even worse.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

germany's foreign minister, **arthur zimmermann**, had sent this message to mexico.


he offered mexico a deal.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

### what happened before that:


in 1916, there were many disturbances along the u.s.–mexico border.


a man named **francisco "pancho" villa** was behind them.


he attacked towns in the united states.


the u.s. army sent thousands of troops to the border.


their job was to track down villa.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

### germany's plan:


germany saw a chance.


it wanted mexico to cause more border trouble if war broke out with the united states.


more trouble would mean more u.s. troops stuck at the border.


those troops could not be sent to fight in europe.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

### the promise made to mexico:


in return, germany made a promise.


if germany won the war, it would give mexico back three regions:


**texas**, **new mexico**, and **arizona**.


mexico had lost that land in 1848 after a war with the united states.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## why mexico could not join


in reality, mexico could not take part in this plan.


even if it wanted to, it was too divided.


in 1917, violent conflict raged among many revolutionary groups inside mexico.


villa's forces were just one of them.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

to understand why, we need to look back at how the trouble started.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## the mexican revolution begins (1910–1913)


in 1910, a liberal politician named **francisco madero** ran for president of mexico.


he challenged **porfirio díaz**.


díaz had held the presidency without a break since 1884.


his rule was like a dictatorship.


it favored wealthy landowners and business owners.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

díaz won the election.


the vote was rigged.


he then had madero arrested.


this act sparked uprisings across the country.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

in 1911, díaz was forced to step down.


he went into exile.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

madero was declared president.


but he failed to deliver the changes people expected.


a new revolt broke out against his government.


in 1913, madero was forced to give up his office.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

a general named **victoriano huerta** replaced him.


huerta commanded rebellious forces within the mexican army.


soon after taking power, he had madero killed.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

huerta tried to rule like a dictator.


opposition rose quickly.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## the factions fight (1913–1914)


### in the south:


**emiliano zapata** led a rebellion.


his followers were indigenous peasants.


they wanted land reform.


they wanted their own plots of soil to farm and live on.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

### in the north:


three men led the revolution:


**venustiano carranza**, **álvaro obregón**, and **pancho villa**.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

### the u.s. gets involved:


u.s. president **woodrow wilson** backed carranza.


he supplied carranza's forces with weapons and ammunition.


wilson also used this moment to order u.s. marines into mexico.


his stated goal was to prevent damage to u.s.-owned property.


in **april 1914**, the u.s. navy took control of the mexican port of **veracruz**.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

### the fighting continues:


throughout the summer of 1914, the rebel groups fought each other.


each leader wanted to replace huerta.


in july, huerta fled into exile.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

carranza declared himself president.


villa and zapata objected.


they did not accept his claim.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## villa's raid and the u.s. response (1916)


in **march 1916**, villa attacked the town of **columbus, new mexico**.


this was payback for u.s. support of carranza.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

wilson responded by sending five thousand troops across the border.


their mission: capture villa.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

villa evaded them.


he kept moving and hiding.


within a year, the u.s. troops had gone home.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

this invasion damaged relations between the united states and carranza.


he refused to accept what he saw as an insult to mexican sovereignty.


he felt his country's right to govern itself was being trampled on.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

by 1917, carranza was firmly in power.


but rebellion continued.


villa led fighters in the northern state of **chihuahua**.


zapata led fighters in **morelos** in the south.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## the u.s. reacts and enters the war


so when germany approached carranza with its offer of an alliance against the united states, he was not in a position to accept it.


he did not have the control or the stability to do so.


his own country was still tearing itself apart.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

### the telegram goes public:


but when the telegram became public, the united states was furious.


the country was not yet at war.


and germany was already planning how to break up its territory.


that felt like a direct threat.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

### money and business push toward war:


american banks had lent money to the european allies.


if germany won, those loans would go unpaid.


so the banks supported u.s. involvement.


they needed their money back.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

businesses saw a different benefit.


they hoped that if the united states helped the allies win, the allies would open up global markets to them.


more trade meant more profit.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

### the decision:


popular support for going to war against germany grew fast.


people were angry and ready.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

in **april 1917**, the united states entered the conflict on the side of the allied powers.


the country was already angry over the sinking of the **lusitania** and the **sussex**.


those ships had carried american lives into the sea.


the zimmermann telegram added fuel to that fire.

# latin american independence


## table of contents

- the struggle for freedom
- brazil's unique path
- outside eyes on south america
- maria dundas graham calcott
- what graham's diaries reveal today



&nbsp;



&nbsp;



## the struggle for freedom



&nbsp;



latin american colonies wanted to **break free**.


they wanted freedom from their parent countries.



&nbsp;



those parent countries ran a **mercantilist system**.


that system blocked the growth of fast-growing colonial states.



&nbsp;



the independence movements were led by **well-educated elites**.


but they often faced resistance.



&nbsp;



a majority of the population was mixed-race.


many of them sided with the homeland.



&nbsp;



this lack of popular support created a problem.



&nbsp;



the white creole minority feared the mixed-race majorities.


they had to **negotiate at every step**.


they could not simply take power by force.



&nbsp;



liberators like **bolívar**, **san martín**, **pedro i**, and **iturbide** led these movements.


they received no significant help from outside sources.


many times they lacked a unified direction or strategy.



&nbsp;



they also faced practical problems:


- vast geographic distances
- natural obstacles (mountains, rivers)
- economic isolation of different regions
- cultural differences between regions



&nbsp;



all of this made it hard to **unite the areas** they were trying to free.



&nbsp;



&nbsp;



---



&nbsp;



&nbsp;



## brazil's unique path



&nbsp;



brazil took a **different path**.


it was different from other latin american countries.



&nbsp;



its independence showed more continuity with its colonial past.


no other nation in the region matched that continuity.



&nbsp;



after 1822, brazil was free of portuguese rule.


but it did not become a republic.


it became an **independent empire**.



&nbsp;



the first emperor was **pedro i**.


he was a prince of the portuguese royal house.


he was still an heir to the portuguese throne.



&nbsp;



portuguese-born men kept their power after independence.


they continued to control trade.


they held key positions in:


- the bureaucracy
- the army
- the church



&nbsp;



political life in imperial brazil was shaped by a **conservative-liberal split**.


this division reflected conflicting interests and ideals.


those conflicts came from different economic groups.



&nbsp;



independence did not bring major changes to colonial socioeconomic structures.


the new constitutional monarchy simply **regularized the status quo**.



&nbsp;



&nbsp;



---



&nbsp;



&nbsp;



## outside eyes on south america



&nbsp;



the independence movement drew great **outside interest** in the region.


that curiosity led to more foreign travel and writing.



&nbsp;



people in the united states and europe often learned about south america through these accounts.


travelers wrote down what they saw.


others read those writings back home.



&nbsp;



in the nineteenth century, european women rarely traveled for pleasure.


tourism was a twentieth-century invention.


so **female travelers were unusual**.



&nbsp;



&nbsp;



---



&nbsp;



&nbsp;



## maria dundas graham calcott



&nbsp;



**maria dundas graham calcott** came to south america in the 1820s.


she traveled with her husband.


he was a navy officer.



&nbsp;



she served as governess to pedro i's daughter.


that daughter was **princess maria da glória**.


she would later become queen of portugal.



&nbsp;



graham kept diaries from **1821 to 1823**.


these diaries offer a unique view of the brazilian court.


they cover the independence process.



&nbsp;



her accounts also show her wide-ranging interest in people's daily lives.


she wrote about:


- brazil (salvador and rio de janeiro)
- chile (santiago)



&nbsp;



in chile, graham studied **science and botany**.


her teacher was thomas cochrane.


he was a former british navy officer.


he was also a friend of the family.



&nbsp;



&nbsp;



---



&nbsp;



&nbsp;



## what graham's diaries reveal today



&nbsp;



graham wrote her reports for her own time.


she wanted to enlighten and entertain her contemporaries.


but **today**, her words offer new insights.



&nbsp;



her diaries reveal two things:


- gender relations in newly independent south american nations
- the elite social circles of the early nineteenth century



&nbsp;



graham focused on what she saw as **"suitable" subjects** for a woman.


these included fashion and motherhood.


they related to the lives of brazilian women.



&nbsp;



public life was largely male.


her diaries show that female foreign observers like herself remained **outsiders** in those spaces.



&nbsp;



graham was an urban elite woman with a protestant background.


in her writing, she held up civilized english customs as a model for the world.


that bias shaped what she noticed and how she described it.



&nbsp;



but despite that bias—or perhaps because of it—her accounts reveal much.



&nbsp;



through her view of private aspects of brazilian women's lives, we learn about **public events** in south american society.


we also see her own models of femininity at work.



&nbsp;



her travel narrative is a window into one woman's world.


it shows how she saw nineteenth-century brazilian women **shape their lives and their environment**.

